data.slides=[{"section":"Launch","title":"1.5 · Exponent Laws and Logarithms","html":"<div class=\"el-cover\">\n  <div class=\"el-cover-copy\">\n    <div class=\"el-kicker\">IB MATHEMATICS: APPLICATIONS AND INTERPRETATION SL · UNIT 1</div>\n    <h1><span>1.5</span> Exponent Laws and Logarithms</h1>\n    <p class=\"el-lead\">Build exact exponent structure, solve unknown-exponent equations, invert multiplicative models with logarithms and interpret logarithmic scales.</p>\n    <div class=\"el-cover-pills\"><span>73 purposeful screens</span><span>96 Practice Studio questions</span><span>14-question quiz</span><span>5 IB tasks</span></div>\n    <div class=\"el-central\"><b>Central idea</b><span>Exponents describe repeated multiplicative change; logarithms reverse that change by answering “what exponent?”</span></div>\n    <button class=\"cover-start\" data-cover-next=\"1\">Begin lesson <span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></button>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-cover-visual\" aria-label=\"Exponent and logarithm inverse pathway\">\n    <div class=\"el-machine-title\">MULTIPLICATIVE CHANGE AND ITS INVERSE</div>\n    <div class=\"el-machine\">\n      <div class=\"el-input\"><small>exponent</small><b>\\(y\\)</b></div>\n      <div class=\"el-process\"><span>raise base \\(b\\)</span><b>\\(b^y\\)</b></div>\n      <div class=\"el-output\"><small>result</small><b>\\(x\\)</b></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"el-inverse-arrow\">reverse with \\(\\log_b\\)</div>\n    <div class=\"el-equivalence\">\\[b^y=x\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\log_b x=y\\]</div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"cover","eyebrow":"Definitive classroom release · v6.0.0"},{"section":"Launch","title":"Opening problem · when will the target be reached?","html":"<div class=\"el-opening\">\n  <div>\n    <div class=\"el-label\">OPENING PROBLEM</div>\n    <p>A culture contains \\(650\\) cells and grows by \\(14\\%\\) each hour. The model is</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[N(t)=650(1.14)^t.\\]</div>\n    <div class=\"el-question-grid\">\n      <div><b>A</b><span>Predict whether the culture reaches \\(3000\\) cells before or after \\(10\\) hours.</span></div>\n      <div><b>B</b><span>Explain why repeated multiplication—not repeated addition—models the situation.</span></div>\n      <div><b>C</b><span>How could you solve \\(650(1.14)^t=3000\\) when \\(t\\) is not an obvious integer?</span></div>\n      <div><b>D</b><span>If observations occur only at whole hours, how should a decimal answer be reported?</span></div>\n    </div>\n    <textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"opening\" aria-label=\"Opening problem response\"></textarea>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-growth-visual\">\n    <div class=\"el-growth-bar\" style=\"--h:18%\"><span>0 h</span><b>650</b></div>\n    <div class=\"el-growth-bar\" style=\"--h:31%\"><span>4 h</span><b>1097</b></div>\n    <div class=\"el-growth-bar\" style=\"--h:52%\"><span>8 h</span><b>1855</b></div>\n    <div class=\"el-growth-bar target\" style=\"--h:84%\"><span>12 h</span><b>3131</b></div>\n    <div class=\"el-target-line\">target \\(=3000\\)</div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"Predict first · calculate later · interpret last"},{"section":"Learning route","title":"Learning intentions and mastery evidence","html":"<div class=\"el-goal-grid\">\n  <article><b>1</b><h3>Use exponent laws</h3><p>Simplify integer, zero, negative and rational powers exactly.</p></article>\n  <article><b>2</b><h3>Solve structurally</h3><p>Use a common base when possible and recognize inconsistent or identity equations.</p></article>\n  <article><b>3</b><h3>Invert with logarithms</h3><p>Translate forms, evaluate logarithms and use change of base.</p></article>\n  <article><b>4</b><h3>Solve models</h3><p>Isolate an exponential expression, apply logs and verify in the original equation.</p></article>\n  <article><b>5</b><h3>Interpret scales</h3><p>Convert additive changes on a logarithmic scale into multiplicative ratios.</p></article>\n  <article><b>6</b><h3>Evaluate conclusions</h3><p>Respect domains, units, discrete timing, assumptions and calculator evidence.</p></article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-success\"><b>Mastery evidence:</b> exact structure → efficient method → independent verification → contextual interpretation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what a complete solution must contain"},{"section":"Diagnostic","title":"Try before instruction","html":"<div class=\"el-diagnostic\">\n  <div class=\"el-question-grid\">\n    <div><b>A</b><span>Simplify \\(a^5a^{-2}\\), for \\(a\\ne0\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>B</b><span>Evaluate \\(27^{2/3}\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>C</b><span>Solve \\(4^x=64\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>D</b><span>Explain what changes when the equation becomes \\(4^x=70\\).</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"diagnostic\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Diagnostic lens</summary><p>\\(a^3\\); \\(9\\); \\(x=3\\). The final equation has no convenient integer exponent, so an inverse operation or graph is needed.</p></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Reveal prior knowledge without grading it"},{"section":"Exponent foundations","title":"What an exponent records","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <div class=\"el-label\">MEANING</div>\n    <p>For a positive integer \\(n\\), the exponent counts equal factors:</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[a^n=\\underbrace{a\\cdot a\\cdots a}_{n\\text{ factors}}.\\]</div>\n    <p>The laws of exponents preserve this multiplicative structure when expressions are compressed.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-factor-stack\">\n    <div><span>\\(a^2\\)</span><i></i><i></i></div>\n    <div><span>\\(a^3\\)</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>\n    <div><span>\\(a^5\\)</span><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Start from repeated multiplication, not memorized slogans"},{"section":"Exponent foundations","title":"The exponent-law map","html":"<div class=\"el-law-grid\">\n  <article><span>PRODUCT</span><div>\\[a^ma^n=a^{m+n}\\]</div><p>Combine equal bases under multiplication.</p></article>\n  <article><span>QUOTIENT</span><div>\\[\\frac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\\]</div><p>Subtract exponents when \\(a\\ne0\\).</p></article>\n  <article><span>POWER OF A POWER</span><div>\\[(a^m)^n=a^{mn}\\]</div><p>Each repeated block contributes \\(m\\) factors.</p></article>\n  <article><span>POWER OF A PRODUCT</span><div>\\[(ab)^n=a^nb^n\\]</div><p>The exponent applies to every factor.</p></article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-warning\"><b>Scope condition:</b> these laws apply to products and quotients. They do not distribute across addition or subtraction.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Four structural moves, each with a condition"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Product law · why exponents add","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>Multiplying \\(a^3\\) by \\(a^4\\) creates seven factors of \\(a\\):</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[a^3a^4=(aaa)(aaaa)=a^7.\\]</div>\n    <p>Therefore the exponent records the total number of equal factors.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-step-visual\">\n    <div><b>\\(a^3\\)</b><span>3 factors</span></div><strong>+</strong><div><b>\\(a^4\\)</b><span>4 factors</span></div><strong>=</strong><div class=\"accent\"><b>\\(a^7\\)</b><span>7 factors</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Derive the law before using it"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Worked example · product law","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 1</span><b>Simplify \\(6x^4y^{-2}\\cdot 3x^{-1}y^5\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Multiply coefficients: \\(6\\cdot3=18\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>Add exponents on \\(x\\): \\(4+(-1)=3\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>Add exponents on \\(y\\): \\(-2+5=3\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[6x^4y^{-2}\\cdot3x^{-1}y^5=18x^3y^3.\\]</div>\n  <div class=\"el-check\"><b>Check:</b> the final expression contains no negative exponent and preserves every factor.</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Separate coefficients from exponent structure"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Student turn · product law","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Simplify \\(4a^{-3}b^2\\cdot 5a^7b^{-5}\\), writing the answer with positive exponents.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"product-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(20a^4b^{-3}=\\dfrac{20a^4}{b^3}\\). Add exponents on like bases, then rewrite the negative exponent as a reciprocal.</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Commit before revealing"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Misconception clinic · addition is not a product","html":"<div class=\"el-misconception\">\n  <div class=\"el-wrong\"><span>FALSE</span>\\[(a+b)^2=a^2+b^2\\]</div>\n  <div class=\"el-repair\">\n    <h3>Counterexample</h3>\n    <p>Let \\(a=b=1\\). Then the left side is \\(4\\), while the right side is \\(2\\).</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2.\\]</div>\n    <p><b>Repair move:</b> only distribute an exponent across factors, not across a sum.</p>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"A single counterexample disproves a universal claim"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Quotient law · why exponents subtract","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>Common factors cancel when the base is non-zero:</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[\\frac{a^7}{a^3}=\\frac{a^3a^4}{a^3}=a^4.\\]</div>\n    <p>The difference \\(7-3\\) counts the factors that remain.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-condition\">\n    <b>Why \\(a\\ne0\\)?</b>\n    <p>A quotient with denominator \\(a^3\\) is undefined when \\(a=0\\).</p>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Cancellation explains subtraction"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Worked example · quotient law","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 2</span><b>Simplify \\(\\dfrac{24p^9q^{-2}}{6p^3q^4}\\), for \\(p,q\\ne0\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Divide coefficients: \\(24/6=4\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(p^{9-3}=p^6\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>\\(q^{-2-4}=q^{-6}=1/q^6\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\frac{24p^9q^{-2}}{6p^3q^4}=\\frac{4p^6}{q^6}.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Subtract denominator exponents, including their signs"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Student turn · quotient law","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Simplify \\(\\dfrac{15x^{-2}y^8}{5x^3y^{-1}}\\), for \\(x,y\\ne0\\).</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"quotient-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(3x^{-5}y^9=\\dfrac{3y^9}{x^5}\\). For \\(x\\), compute \\(-2-3=-5\\); for \\(y\\), compute \\(8-(-1)=9\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Track subtraction signs explicitly"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Power of a power","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>Raising \\(a^m\\) to power \\(n\\) repeats the entire \\(m\\)-factor block \\(n\\) times:</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[(a^m)^n=\\underbrace{a^m\\cdot a^m\\cdots a^m}_{n\\text{ blocks}}=a^{mn}.\\]</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-step-visual\">\n    <div><b>\\((a^3)^4\\)</b><span>4 blocks of 3</span></div><strong>→</strong><div class=\"accent\"><b>\\(a^{12}\\)</b><span>\\(3\\times4\\)</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Multiply exponents because whole blocks repeat"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Worked example · nested powers","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 3</span><b>Simplify \\(\\left(\\dfrac{2a^3}{b^{-2}}\\right)^3\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Apply the outer power to every factor.</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(2^3=8\\), \\((a^3)^3=a^9\\), and \\((b^{-2})^3=b^{-6}\\) remains in the denominator.</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>Dividing by \\(b^{-6}\\) is multiplying by \\(b^6\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\left(\\frac{2a^3}{b^{-2}}\\right)^3=8a^9b^6.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Apply the exponent to the complete product or quotient"},{"section":"Exponent laws","title":"Student turn · nested powers","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Simplify \\(\\left(3m^{-2}n^4\\right)^2\\) with positive exponents.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"power-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(9m^{-4}n^8=\\dfrac{9n^8}{m^4}\\). The coefficient is squared and each exponent is multiplied by \\(2\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Do not forget the numerical coefficient"},{"section":"Zero and negative exponents","title":"Why \\(a^0=1\\)","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>For \\(a\\ne0\\), divide a power by itself in two valid ways:</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[\\frac{a^m}{a^m}=1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\frac{a^m}{a^m}=a^{m-m}=a^0.\\]</div>\n    <p>Therefore \\(a^0=1\\) for \\(a\\ne0\\).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-condition\">\n    <b>Important</b>\n    <p>The reasoning requires division by \\(a^m\\), so it does not define \\(0^0\\).</p>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"A quotient identity creates the zero exponent"},{"section":"Zero and negative exponents","title":"Negative exponents are reciprocals","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>Continue the exponent pattern by dividing by \\(a\\):</p>\n    <div class=\"el-sequence\">\\[a^2,\\ a^1,\\ a^0,\\ a^{-1},\\ a^{-2}\\]</div>\n    <div class=\"el-sequence-labels\"><span>\\(\\div a\\)</span><span>\\(\\div a\\)</span><span>\\(\\div a\\)</span><span>\\(\\div a\\)</span></div>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[a^{-n}=\\frac1{a^n},\\qquad a\\ne0.\\]</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-example-card\"><b>Example</b><p>\\(5^{-3}=1/5^3=1/125\\).</p></div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The minus sign changes location, not the sign of the value"},{"section":"Zero and negative exponents","title":"Worked example · exact reciprocal structure","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 4</span><b>Evaluate \\(\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^{-3}\\) exactly.</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>A negative exponent takes the reciprocal.</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(\\left(\\dfrac35\\right)^{-3}=\\left(\\dfrac53\\right)^3\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>Cube numerator and denominator.</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\left(\\frac35\\right)^{-3}=\\frac{125}{27}.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Reciprocate first, then apply the positive power"},{"section":"Zero and negative exponents","title":"Student turn · negative powers","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Evaluate \\(\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4}\\right)^{-1/2}\\) exactly.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"negative-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>The square root of \\(25/4\\) is \\(5/2\\). The negative exponent then gives the reciprocal, so the answer is \\(2/5\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Root and reciprocal can be applied in either valid order"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Roots written as powers","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>A rational exponent combines a root and a power:</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[a^{m/n}=\\sqrt[n]{a^m}=\\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^m.\\]</div>\n    <p>The denominator gives the root; the numerator gives the power.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-root-map\">\n    <div><b>\\(a^{1/2}\\)</b><span>square root</span></div>\n    <div><b>\\(a^{1/3}\\)</b><span>cube root</span></div>\n    <div><b>\\(a^{3/4}\\)</b><span>fourth root, then cube</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The fraction in the exponent is an instruction"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Choose an efficient order","html":"<div class=\"el-method-choice\">\n  <div><span>ROOT FIRST</span><div>\\[64^{2/3}=(\\sqrt[3]{64})^2=4^2=16\\]</div></div>\n  <div><span>POWER FIRST</span><div>\\[64^{2/3}=\\sqrt[3]{64^2}=\\sqrt[3]{4096}=16\\]</div></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\"><b>Efficiency:</b> root first is usually easier when the base is a perfect \\(n\\)-th power.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Equivalent definitions can have very different workloads"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Worked example · negative rational exponent","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 5</span><b>Evaluate \\(27^{-4/3}\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Take the cube root: \\(27^{1/3}=3\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>Raise to the fourth power: \\(3^4=81\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>Use the negative exponent: take the reciprocal.</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[27^{-4/3}=\\frac1{81}.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Denominator → root; numerator → power; negative → reciprocal"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Student turn · exact value","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Evaluate \\(16^{-3/4}\\) exactly.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"rational-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(\\sqrt[4]{16}=2\\), so \\(16^{3/4}=2^3=8\\). The negative exponent gives \\(1/8\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Keep the value exact"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Real-domain restrictions","html":"<div class=\"el-domain-grid\">\n  <article><span>ODD ROOT</span><div>\\[\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\]</div><p>Odd roots of negative real numbers are real.</p></article>\n  <article><span>EVEN ROOT</span><div>\\[\\sqrt{-8}\\notin\\mathbb R\\]</div><p>Even roots require a non-negative radicand in the real number system.</p></article>\n  <article><span>NEGATIVE POWER</span><div>\\[a^{-n}=\\frac1{a^n}\\]</div><p>The base cannot be zero.</p></article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-warning\"><b>Calculator errors carry mathematical meaning:</b> check the domain before treating an error as a device problem.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"A valid algebraic form still needs a valid domain"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Worked example · simplify exactly","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 6</span><b>Simplify \\(\\dfrac{32^{3/5}\\cdot 8^{-2/3}}{4^{-1}}\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>\\(32^{3/5}=(2^5)^{3/5}=2^3=8\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(8^{-2/3}=(2^3)^{-2/3}=2^{-2}=1/4\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>Dividing by \\(4^{-1}=1/4\\) multiplies by \\(4\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\frac{8\\cdot(1/4)}{1/4}=8.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Rewrite bases as powers before using decimals"},{"section":"Rational exponents","title":"Student turn · combine exact structures","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Simplify \\(81^{3/4}\\cdot 9^{-1/2}\\) exactly.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"exact-combine\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(81^{3/4}=(3^4)^{3/4}=27\\) and \\(9^{-1/2}=1/3\\). The product is \\(9\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Look for common prime bases"},{"section":"Checkpoint","title":"Exponent-law mastery checkpoint","html":"<div class=\"el-checkpoint\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">CHECKPOINT 1</div>\n  <div class=\"el-question-grid\">\n    <div><b>A</b><span>Simplify \\(\\dfrac{(x^3)^4x^{-5}}{x^2}\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>B</b><span>Evaluate \\(125^{-2/3}\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>C</b><span>State the restriction in \\(\\dfrac{a^{-2}}{a^3}\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>D</b><span>Disprove \\((a-b)^2=a^2-b^2\\).</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"checkpoint-1\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal checkpoint</summary><p>A: \\(x^5\\). B: \\(1/25\\). C: \\(a\\ne0\\). D: for \\(a=2,b=1\\), the two sides are \\(1\\) and \\(3\\).</p></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Independent evidence before equations"},{"section":"Exponential equations","title":"Solve by creating a common base","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>If both sides are powers of the same positive base other than \\(1\\), equal outputs imply equal exponents:</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[a^{f(x)}=a^{g(x)}\\Longrightarrow f(x)=g(x),\\qquad a\\gt 0,\\ a\\ne1.\\]</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-decision\">\n    <b>First question</b>\n    <p>Can both sides be rewritten using one base?</p>\n    <span>YES → compare exponents</span>\n    <span>NO → isolate and use logarithms or a graph</span>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Structure before technology"},{"section":"Exponential equations","title":"Worked example · common base","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 7</span><b>Solve \\(4^{3x-1}=8^{x+2}\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Rewrite \\(4=2^2\\) and \\(8=2^3\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(2^{6x-2}=2^{3x+6}\\), so \\(6x-2=3x+6\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>\\(3x=8\\), hence \\(x=8/3\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[x=\\frac83.\\]</div>\n  <div class=\"el-check\"><b>Verification:</b> substitute \\(x=8/3\\); both exponents become \\(7\\), giving \\(2^7\\).</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Make bases equivalent before equating exponents"},{"section":"Exponential equations","title":"Student turn · common base","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Solve \\(\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{3x-1}=4^{x+2}\\).</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"common-base-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>Write both sides in base \\(2\\): \\(2^{-3x+1}=2^{2x+4}\\). Hence \\(-3x+1=2x+4\\), so \\(x=-3/5\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"A reciprocal base changes the sign of the exponent"},{"section":"Exponential equations","title":"No solution and every real number","html":"<div class=\"el-case-grid\">\n  <article>\n    <span>INCONSISTENT</span>\n    <div>\\[2^{3x-1}=8^{x+2}\\]</div>\n    <p>Base \\(2\\) gives \\(3x-1=3x+6\\), or \\(-1=6\\). There is no solution.</p>\n  </article>\n  <article>\n    <span>IDENTITY</span>\n    <div>\\[4^{x+1}=2^{2x+2}\\]</div>\n    <p>The two sides are identical for every real \\(x\\), so all real numbers solve the equation.</p>\n  </article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-warning\"><b>Do not force a numerical answer:</b> compare the resulting equations honestly.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Common-base equations can reveal structure immediately"},{"section":"Exponential equations","title":"Worked example · factor an exponential expression","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 8</span><b>Solve \\(2^x+2^{x+1}=96\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Use \\(2^{x+1}=2\\cdot2^x\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>Factor: \\(2^x(1+2)=96\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>\\(2^x=32=2^5\\), so \\(x=5\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[x=5.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Treat \\(2^x\\) as a common factor"},{"section":"Exponential equations","title":"Student turn · substitute a repeated exponential","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Solve \\(9^x-10\\cdot3^x+9=0\\).</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"substitution-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>Let \\(u=3^x\\gt 0\\). Then \\(9^x=u^2\\), so \\(u^2-10u+9=0\\), giving \\(u=1\\) or \\(u=9\\). Hence \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"A repeated exponential can behave like a quadratic variable"},{"section":"Transition","title":"When a common base is not available","html":"<div class=\"el-transition\">\n  <div class=\"el-known\"><b>Easy to invert</b><div>\\[2^x=32\\Longrightarrow x=5\\]</div></div>\n  <div class=\"el-unknown\"><b>Needs a new inverse</b><div>\\[2^x=30\\Longrightarrow x=?\\]</div></div>\n  <div class=\"el-scale\"><span>\\(2^4=16\\)</span><i></i><span>\\(30\\)</span><i></i><span>\\(2^5=32\\)</span></div>\n  <p>The unknown exponent lies between \\(4\\) and \\(5\\). A logarithm gives its exact inverse form and a calculator gives a decimal approximation.</p>\n</div>","kind":"inquiry","eyebrow":"A mathematical need introduces the logarithm"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"A logarithm answers “what exponent?”","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[b^y=x\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\log_bx=y.\\]</div>\n    <p>The base \\(b\\) must satisfy \\(b\\gt 0\\) and \\(b\\ne1\\). The argument \\(x\\) must be positive.</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-anatomy\">\n    <div class=\"el-log-expression\"><span class=\"base\">\\(b\\)</span><span class=\"log\">log</span><span class=\"argument\">\\(x\\)</span><span class=\"equals\">\\(=y\\)</span></div>\n    <div class=\"el-anatomy-labels\"><span>base</span><span>argument</span><span>exponent</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Definition, conditions and meaning in one equivalence"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Translate between the two forms","html":"<div class=\"el-translation\">\n  <div><span>EXPONENTIAL FORM</span><div>\\[5^3=125\\]</div></div>\n  <div class=\"el-double-arrow\">⇄</div>\n  <div><span>LOGARITHMIC FORM</span><div>\\[\\log_5(125)=3\\]</div></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\"><b>Reading:</b> “the logarithm base \\(5\\) of \\(125\\) is \\(3\\)” means “\\(5\\) raised to \\(3\\) equals \\(125\\).”</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The base stays the base; the exponent becomes the answer"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Worked example · translate both directions","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 9</span><b>Translate each statement.</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>A</b><p>\\(10^{-4}=0.0001\\Longleftrightarrow \\log_{10}(0.0001)=-4\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>B</b><p>\\(\\log_3(1/27)=-3\\Longleftrightarrow3^{-3}=1/27\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-check\"><b>Check:</b> the base remains unchanged, the result and exponent exchange roles.</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Use the equivalence, not a memorized word order"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Student turn · translate","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Write \\(\\log_2(64)=6\\) in exponential form, and write \\(7^{-2}=1/49\\) in logarithmic form.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"translate-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(2^6=64\\) and \\(\\log_7(1/49)=-2\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Track base, exponent and result"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Special logarithm values","html":"<div class=\"el-special-grid\">\n  <article><div>\\[\\log_b1=0\\]</div><p>because \\(b^0=1\\)</p></article>\n  <article><div>\\[\\log_bb=1\\]</div><p>because \\(b^1=b\\)</p></article>\n  <article><div>\\[\\log_b(b^k)=k\\]</div><p>inverse operations undo each other</p></article>\n  <article><div>\\[b^{\\log_bx}=x\\]</div><p>for \\(x\\gt 0\\)</p></article>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Inverse identities should be recognized instantly"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Common and natural logarithms","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-definition-card\"><span>COMMON LOGARITHM</span><div>\\[\\log x=\\log_{10}x\\]</div><p>Base \\(10\\); common on scientific scales.</p></div>\n  <div class=\"el-definition-card\"><span>NATURAL LOGARITHM</span><div>\\[\\ln x=\\log_ex\\]</div><p>Base \\(e\\approx2.71828\\); natural for continuous exponential models.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\"><b>Both are valid inverse tools:</b> the choice of \\(\\log\\) or \\(\\ln\\) does not change a correctly solved exponential equation.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Know what each calculator key means"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Change of base","html":"<div class=\"el-derivation\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">DERIVATION</div>\n  <p>Let \\(y=\\log_bx\\). Then \\(b^y=x\\). Taking natural logs gives</p>\n  <div class=\"el-display\">\\[\\ln(b^y)=\\ln x\\Longrightarrow y\\ln b=\\ln x.\\]</div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\boxed{\\log_bx=\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln b}=\\frac{\\log x}{\\log b}}\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Any valid logarithm can evaluate any other valid base"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Worked example · evaluate a non-standard base","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 10</span><b>Evaluate \\(\\log_3(20)\\) to three decimal places.</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Use change of base: \\(\\log_3(20)=\\dfrac{\\ln20}{\\ln3}\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>Enter the complete quotient with brackets.</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>\\(\\log_3(20)\\approx2.727\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-check\"><b>Reasonableness:</b> \\(3^2=9\\) and \\(3^3=27\\), so the answer must lie between \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Estimate before trusting a decimal"},{"section":"Logarithms","title":"Student turn · change of base","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Evaluate \\(\\log_7(100)\\) to three decimal places and justify its interval before calculating.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"change-base-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(7^2=49\\) and \\(7^3=343\\), so the value is between \\(2\\) and \\(3\\). Change of base gives \\(\\log(100)/\\log(7)\\approx2.367\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Interval first, calculator second"},{"section":"Logarithm laws","title":"Products become sums","html":"<div class=\"el-law-grid log-laws\">\n  <article><span>PRODUCT</span><div>\\[\\log_b(MN)=\\log_bM+\\log_bN\\]</div></article>\n  <article><span>QUOTIENT</span><div>\\[\\log_b\\left(\\frac MN\\right)=\\log_bM-\\log_bN\\]</div></article>\n  <article><span>POWER</span><div>\\[\\log_b(M^k)=k\\log_bM\\]</div></article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\"><b>Conditions:</b> \\(M\\gt 0\\), \\(N\\gt 0\\), \\(b\\gt 0\\), \\(b\\ne1\\). These laws come from the corresponding exponent laws.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Logarithms convert multiplicative structure into additive structure"},{"section":"Logarithm laws","title":"Worked example · use known logarithms","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 11</span><b>Given \\(\\log A=2.3\\) and \\(\\log B=0.8\\), find \\(\\log(A^2/B)\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Power law: \\(\\log(A^2)=2\\log A=4.6\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>Quotient law: \\(\\log(A^2/B)=4.6-0.8\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\log(A^2/B)=3.8.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Expose the multiplicative structure before substituting values"},{"section":"Logarithm laws","title":"Student turn · combine laws","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>Given \\(\\ln p=1.2\\) and \\(\\ln q=-0.4\\), find \\(\\ln(p^3q^2)\\).</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"log-law-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(\\ln(p^3q^2)=3\\ln p+2\\ln q=3(1.2)+2(-0.4)=2.8\\).</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Coefficients come from exponents"},{"section":"Logarithm laws","title":"Domain of a logarithm","html":"<div class=\"el-domain-line\">\n  <div class=\"el-domain-rule\"><b>REAL LOGARITHM</b><div>\\[\\log_b(f(x))\\text{ exists only when }f(x)\\gt 0.\\]</div></div>\n  <div class=\"el-domain-example\"><span>Example</span><div>\\[\\log(3x-12)\\]</div><p>Require \\(3x-12\\gt 0\\), so \\(x\\gt 4\\).</p></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-warning\"><b>Boundary values are excluded:</b> \\(\\log 0\\) is undefined.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The argument—not the whole expression—must be positive"},{"section":"Logarithm laws","title":"Misconception clinic · logs do not split sums","html":"<div class=\"el-misconception\">\n  <div class=\"el-wrong\"><span>FALSE</span>\\[\\log(a+b)=\\log a+\\log b\\]</div>\n  <div class=\"el-repair\">\n    <h3>Numerical test</h3>\n    <p>For \\(a=20\\), \\(b=30\\):</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[\\log50\\approx1.699,\\qquad \\log20+\\log30\\approx2.778.\\]</div>\n    <p>The logarithm laws apply to products, quotients and powers—not to addition inside the argument.</p>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"misconception","eyebrow":"Test a claimed identity with simple positive values"},{"section":"Solving with logarithms","title":"The general solving route","html":"<div class=\"el-workflow\">\n  <div><b>1</b><span>Define the variable and units</span></div>\n  <div><b>2</b><span>Isolate the exponential expression</span></div>\n  <div><b>3</b><span>Take \\(\\log\\) or \\(\\ln\\) of both sides</span></div>\n  <div><b>4</b><span>Use the power law to bring down the exponent</span></div>\n  <div><b>5</b><span>Solve and keep guard digits</span></div>\n  <div><b>6</b><span>Substitute, interpret and round</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-display\">\\[ab^x=c\\Longrightarrow x=\\frac{\\ln(c/a)}{\\ln b},\\quad a,c\\gt 0,\\ b\\gt 0,\\ b\\ne1.\\]</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"A reproducible six-step method"},{"section":"Solving with logarithms","title":"Worked example · growth target","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 12</span><b>Solve \\(650(1.14)^t=3000\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>\\((1.14)^t=3000/650\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(t\\ln(1.14)=\\ln(3000/650)\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>\\(t=\\dfrac{\\ln(3000/650)}{\\ln(1.14)}\\approx11.672\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[t\\approx11.7\\text{ hours}\\]</div>\n  <div class=\"el-check\"><b>Verification:</b> substituting \\(t=11.672\\) gives approximately \\(3000\\).</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Isolate before taking logarithms"},{"section":"Solving with logarithms","title":"Student turn · decay target","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>A medicine follows \\(M(t)=1200(0.92)^t\\). Find when \\(M(t)=700\\), to two decimal places.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"decay-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>\\(0.92^t=700/1200\\), so \\(t=\\ln(700/1200)/\\ln(0.92)\\approx6.46\\). The model reaches \\(700\\) units after about \\(6.46\\) time units.</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"A decay base gives a negative denominator and a positive time"},{"section":"Solving with logarithms","title":"Natural exponential models","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <p>Continuous exponential models often use base \\(e\\):</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[Q(t)=Q_0e^{kt}.\\]</div>\n    <p>Taking natural logs gives \\(\\ln(Q/Q_0)=kt\\).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-parameter-grid\">\n    <div><b>\\(k\\gt 0\\)</b><span>continuous growth</span></div>\n    <div><b>\\(k\\lt 0\\)</b><span>continuous decay</span></div>\n    <div><b>\\(k=0\\)</b><span>constant quantity</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The sign of \\(k\\) controls the direction of change"},{"section":"Solving with logarithms","title":"Worked example · continuous rate","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 13</span><b>A population doubles every \\(7.5\\) years. Find \\(k\\) in \\(P=P_0e^{kt}\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Doubling means \\(2P_0=P_0e^{7.5k}\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>\\(2=e^{7.5k}\\), so \\(\\ln2=7.5k\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>\\(k=\\ln2/7.5\\approx0.09242\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[k\\approx0.0924\\text{ year}^{-1}.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"A rate constant carries reciprocal-time units"},{"section":"Method selection","title":"Common base, logarithms or graph?","html":"<div class=\"el-decision-tree\">\n  <div class=\"root\">EXponential equation</div>\n  <div class=\"branch\">\n    <article><b>Can bases be matched exactly?</b><span>Use a common base and compare exponents.</span><small>Example: \\(9^{x-1}=3^{x+3}\\)</small></article>\n    <article><b>Can the exponential be isolated?</b><span>Use logarithms and the power law.</span><small>Example: \\(120(1.08)^t=500\\)</small></article>\n    <article><b>Is the equation structurally mixed?</b><span>Use substitution or graph intersection, then verify.</span><small>Example: \\(9^x-10\\cdot3^x+9=0\\)</small></article>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Choose the method from the equation’s structure"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Graph intersection as independent verification","html":"<div class=\"el-graph-card\">\n  <div class=\"el-graph-toolbar\"><span>\\(y_1=650(1.14)^x\\)</span><span>\\(y_2=3000\\)</span></div>\n  <div class=\"el-graph\">\n    <div class=\"axis-x\"></div><div class=\"axis-y\"></div>\n    <div class=\"growth-curve\"></div><div class=\"target-curve\"></div><div class=\"intersection-dot\"></div>\n    <div class=\"intersection-label\">\\((11.67,3000)\\)</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-graph-check\"><b>Agreement:</b> algebraic logarithm method and graph intersection should match within rounding.</div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Technology validates reasoning; it does not replace the model"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Transparent GDC workflow","html":"<div class=\"el-evidence-grid\">\n  <article><b>MODEL</b><p>Record the equation and define each variable.</p></article>\n  <article><b>ENTRY</b><p>Use brackets around complete numerators and denominators.</p></article>\n  <article><b>OUTPUT</b><p>Keep guard digits until the final contextual rounding.</p></article>\n  <article><b>CHECK</b><p>Substitute or compare with an independent graph.</p></article>\n  <article><b>INTERPRET</b><p>State units and whether time is continuous or discrete.</p></article>\n  <article><b>EVALUATE</b><p>State one assumption or limitation of the model.</p></article>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Calculator evidence must be reproducible"},{"section":"Technology","title":"Interactive exponent–logarithm explorer","html":"<div id=\"lesson-lab\" class=\"lab el-lab\" data-lab=\"1.5\"></div>\n<div class=\"el-lab-prompts\">\n  <div><b>Predict</b><span>How does the solution change when the growth factor moves closer to \\(1\\)?</span></div>\n  <div><b>Compare</b><span>Use both algebraic and graphical methods.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Explain</b><span>Which quantities remain invariant under change of logarithm base?</span></div>\n</div>","kind":"lab","eyebrow":"Change one input at a time, then explain the pattern"},{"section":"Thresholds","title":"Continuous crossing versus recorded period","html":"<div class=\"el-threshold\">\n  <div class=\"el-time-line\">\n    <div style=\"--p:0%\"><b>11</b><span>\\(N(11)=2745\\)</span></div>\n    <div class=\"crossing\" style=\"--p:67%\"><b>11.67</b><span>continuous crossing</span></div>\n    <div class=\"reported\" style=\"--p:100%\"><b>12</b><span>\\(N(12)=3131\\)</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-threshold-rule\"><b>First whole period above a target</b><p>Find the continuous crossing, then test the adjacent admissible integer values. Do not round mechanically.</p></div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Discrete reporting is a modelling decision"},{"section":"Thresholds","title":"Worked example · first complete period","html":"<div class=\"el-worked\">\n  <div class=\"el-worked-head\"><span>WORKED EXAMPLE 14</span><b>\\(P(t)=12000(0.97)^t\\). Find the first whole year with \\(P\\lt 9000\\).</b></div>\n  <div class=\"el-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><p>Solve \\(12000(0.97)^t=9000\\): \\(t\\approx9.445\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><p>Check year \\(9\\): \\(P(9)\\approx9123\\gt 9000\\).</p></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><p>Check year \\(10\\): \\(P(10)\\approx8849\\lt 9000\\).</p></div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-result\">\\[\\text{The first whole year below }9000\\text{ is year }10.\\]</div>\n</div>","kind":"worked","eyebrow":"Boundary plus adjacent-value evidence"},{"section":"Thresholds","title":"Student turn · discrete observation","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>A model reaches a target at \\(t=8.12\\) months and is increasing. Observations are recorded at the end of each whole month. State the first recorded month at or above the target and justify.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"threshold-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>Month \\(9\\). The continuous crossing lies between months \\(8\\) and \\(9\\); an adjacent-value check should show month \\(8\\) below and month \\(9\\) at or above the target.</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Justification requires the neighbouring admissible values"},{"section":"Logarithmic scales","title":"Equal scale steps represent equal ratios","html":"<div class=\"el-log-scale\">\n  <div><span>0</span><b>\\(1\\)</b></div>\n  <div><span>1</span><b>\\(10\\)</b></div>\n  <div><span>2</span><b>\\(100\\)</b></div>\n  <div><span>3</span><b>\\(1000\\)</b></div>\n  <div><span>4</span><b>\\(10000\\)</b></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\"><b>Base-10 scale:</b> adding \\(1\\) to the scale value multiplies the underlying quantity by \\(10\\). Equal distances encode equal ratios, not equal differences.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Logarithms compress wide multiplicative ranges"},{"section":"Logarithmic scales","title":"Sound intensity and decibels","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[L=10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I}{I_0}\\right)\\text{ dB}\\]</div>\n    <p>A level difference \\(\\Delta L\\) corresponds to the intensity ratio</p>\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[\\frac{I_2}{I_1}=10^{\\Delta L/10}.\\]</div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-scale-example\">\n    <div><b>\\(+10\\text{ dB}\\)</b><span>\\(\\times10\\) intensity</span></div>\n    <div><b>\\(+20\\text{ dB}\\)</b><span>\\(\\times100\\) intensity</span></div>\n    <div><b>\\(+30\\text{ dB}\\)</b><span>\\(\\times1000\\) intensity</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The factor \\(10\\) outside the logarithm changes the interpretation"},{"section":"Logarithmic scales","title":"Acidity and pH","html":"<div class=\"el-two\">\n  <div class=\"el-concept\">\n    <div class=\"el-display\">\\[\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log_{10}[H^+].\\]</div>\n    <p>A decrease of \\(1\\) pH unit means the hydrogen-ion concentration is multiplied by \\(10\\).</p>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"el-ph-scale\">\n    <div><b>pH 3</b><span>\\([H^+]=10^{-3}\\)</span></div>\n    <div><b>pH 4</b><span>\\([H^+]=10^{-4}\\)</span></div>\n    <div><b>pH 5</b><span>\\([H^+]=10^{-5}\\)</span></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-warning\"><b>Direction matters:</b> lower pH corresponds to greater \\([H^+]\\).</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"The negative sign reverses the direction of the scale"},{"section":"Logarithmic scales","title":"Student turn · interpret scale differences","html":"<div class=\"el-student\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">STUDENT TURN</div>\n  <p>(a) Two sound levels differ by \\(18\\) dB. Find the intensity ratio. (b) One solution has pH \\(2\\) lower than another. Compare their hydrogen-ion concentrations.</p>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"scale-turn\" aria-label=\"Student response\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and reasoning</summary><div class=\"el-answer\"><p>(a) \\(10^{18/10}=10^{1.8}\\approx63.1\\). (b) The lower-pH solution has \\(10^2=100\\) times the hydrogen-ion concentration.</p></div></details>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Translate additive scale changes into multiplicative ratios"},{"section":"Modelling","title":"Interpret parameters and assumptions","html":"<div class=\"el-model-grid\">\n  <article><span>INITIAL VALUE</span><div>\\[Q(0)=Q_0\\]</div><p>Starting amount and its units.</p></article>\n  <article><span>GROWTH FACTOR</span><div>\\[b=1+r\\]</div><p>Multiplication per discrete time step.</p></article>\n  <article><span>RATE CONSTANT</span><div>\\[k\\]</div><p>Continuous proportional rate with reciprocal-time units.</p></article>\n  <article><span>VALIDITY</span><div>\\[t\\in\\text{model interval}\\]</div><p>Conditions may change outside the observed range.</p></article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\"><b>Evaluation:</b> exponential growth cannot continue indefinitely when resources, capacity or behaviour change.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"A formula is a model with assumptions, not a permanent law"},{"section":"Inquiry","title":"Mini-investigation · linearize exponential data","html":"<div class=\"el-investigation\">\n  <div class=\"el-label\">MINI-INVESTIGATION</div>\n  <h3>Can a logarithm reveal a hidden constant growth factor?</h3>\n  <p>Collect or simulate data following \\(y=Ab^t\\). Construct a table of \\(t\\), \\(y\\) and \\(\\log y\\).</p>\n  <div class=\"el-investigation-steps\">\n    <div><b>1</b><span>Plot \\(y\\) against \\(t\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><span>Plot \\(\\log y\\) against \\(t\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><span>Explain why \\(\\log y=\\log A+t\\log b\\) is linear.</span></div>\n    <div><b>4</b><span>Use the slope to estimate \\(b\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>5</b><span>Comment on residuals and model stability.</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"investigation\"></textarea>\n</div>","kind":"investigation","eyebrow":"Question → transformation → evidence → conclusion"},{"section":"Synthesis","title":"Mixed method studio","html":"<div class=\"el-mixed-grid\">\n  <article><b>A</b><p>Simplify \\(32^{3/5}\\cdot 4^{-1/2}\\).</p><span>exact exponent structure</span></article>\n  <article><b>B</b><p>Solve \\(25^{x+1}=5^{3x-2}\\).</p><span>common base</span></article>\n  <article><b>C</b><p>Solve \\(300(1.07)^t=1000\\).</p><span>logarithmic inversion</span></article>\n  <article><b>D</b><p>Interpret a \\(15\\) dB increase.</p><span>logarithmic scale</span></article>\n  <article><b>E</b><p>State the domain of \\(\\log(5-2x)\\).</p><span>argument restriction</span></article>\n  <article><b>F</b><p>Explain one limitation of an unrestricted growth model.</p><span>model evaluation</span></article>\n</div>\n<textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"mixed-synthesis\"></textarea>\n<details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal concise answers</summary><p>A: \\(4\\). B: \\(x=4\\). C: \\(t=\\ln(10/3)/\\ln1.07\\approx17.79\\). D: factor \\(10^{1.5}\\approx31.6\\). E: \\(x\\lt 2.5\\). F: resources or capacity eventually change the growth factor.</p></details>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Select the method before calculating"},{"section":"Assessment literacy","title":"IB command terms for this lesson","html":"<div class=\"el-command-grid\">\n  <article><b>Write down</b><span>Give a direct exact value or form.</span></article>\n  <article><b>Calculate</b><span>Show reproducible mathematical working.</span></article>\n  <article><b>Determine</b><span>Reach a result with supporting reasoning.</span></article>\n  <article><b>Explain</b><span>State why a method, law or interpretation is valid.</span></article>\n  <article><b>Interpret</b><span>Translate a parameter or result back into context.</span></article>\n  <article><b>Comment</b><span>Make a judgement supported by model evidence.</span></article>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-warning\"><b>Exam communication:</b> a calculator decimal without the model, method, units and interpretation is incomplete.</div>","kind":"content","eyebrow":"Command terms determine the required evidence"},{"section":"Assessment","title":"IB task preview · culture threshold","html":"<div class=\"el-task-preview\">\n  <div class=\"el-task-meta\"><span>Paper 2-style</span><span>11 marks</span><span>GDC expected</span></div>\n  <h3>Culture threshold</h3>\n  <p>A culture is modelled by \\(N(t)=650(1.14)^t\\), where \\(t\\) is measured in hours.</p>\n  <div><b>(a)</b><span>Calculate \\(N(6)\\). <i>[2]</i></span></div>\n  <div><b>(b)</b><span>Determine when \\(N(t)=3000\\). <i>[3]</i></span></div>\n  <div><b>(c)</b><span>Find the first complete hour when the model exceeds \\(3000\\). <i>[2]</i></span></div>\n  <div><b>(d)</b><span>Interpret the factor \\(1.14\\). <i>[2]</i></span></div>\n  <div><b>(e)</b><span>State one limitation of the model. <i>[2]</i></span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"el-note\">The full assessment route contains five independent IB-style tasks with complete markschemes.</div>","kind":"exam","eyebrow":"Preview the expected depth of reasoning"},{"section":"Connections","title":"Where exponentials and logarithms connect","html":"<div class=\"el-connection-grid\">\n  <article><b>SEQUENCES</b><span>Geometric change uses repeated multiplication.</span></article>\n  <article><b>FINANCE</b><span>Compound growth and depreciation require inverse target-time calculations.</span></article>\n  <article><b>FUNCTIONS</b><span>Exponential and logarithmic functions are inverses.</span></article>\n  <article><b>SCIENCE</b><span>pH, sound intensity and other scales compress large ranges.</span></article>\n  <article><b>STATISTICS</b><span>Log transformations can linearize multiplicative relationships.</span></article>\n  <article><b>CALCULUS</b><span>Base \\(e\\) produces natural continuous growth and decay.</span></article>\n</div>\n<textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"connections\" placeholder=\"Describe one connection that changed how you understand the topic.\"></textarea>","kind":"reflection","eyebrow":"Connect ideas across the IB course"},{"section":"Mastery","title":"What strong evidence now looks like","html":"<div class=\"el-mastery-grid\">\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-reflect=\"exact\"><span><b>Exact structure</b>I can simplify exponents without premature decimals.</span></label>\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-reflect=\"method\"><span><b>Method selection</b>I can choose common base, logs, substitution or graph.</span></label>\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-reflect=\"domain\"><span><b>Domain</b>I check bases, arguments, denominators and real roots.</span></label>\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-reflect=\"verify\"><span><b>Verification</b>I substitute or use an independent representation.</span></label>\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-reflect=\"interpret\"><span><b>Interpretation</b>I report units, timing and scale meaning.</span></label>\n  <label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-reflect=\"evaluate\"><span><b>Evaluation</b>I state assumptions and limits of a model.</span></label>\n</div>\n<textarea class=\"student-note\" data-note=\"mastery-reflection\" placeholder=\"Which evidence is strongest? Which requires more practice?\"></textarea>","kind":"reflection","eyebrow":"Mastery requires independent evidence, not screen completion"},{"section":"Exit","title":"Exit ticket and next route","html":"<div class=\"el-exit\">\n  <div class=\"el-question-grid\">\n    <div><b>1</b><span>Simplify \\(16^{-3/4}\\).</span></div>\n    <div><b>2</b><span>Solve \\(7^x=90\\) to three decimal places.</span></div>\n    <div><b>3</b><span>Explain why \\(\\log(-4)\\) is not real.</span></div>\n    <div><b>4</b><span>Interpret a \\(20\\) dB increase.</span></div>\n  </div>\n  <textarea class=\"student-note tall\" data-note=\"exit-ticket\"></textarea>\n  <details class=\"solution-reveal\"><summary>Reveal exit check</summary><p>\\(1/8\\); \\(x=\\ln90/\\ln7\\approx2.312\\); a positive real base raised to a real power cannot produce a negative result; the intensity is multiplied by \\(100\\).</p></details>\n  <div class=\"el-route-actions\">\n    <button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"practice\">Open Practice Studio</button>\n    <button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"exam\">Open IB Tasks</button>\n    <button class=\"route-jump\" data-go=\"quiz\">Start Timed Quiz</button>\n  </div>\n</div>","kind":"student","eyebrow":"Calculate · explain · interpret · continue"}];
data.practice=[{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F01","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Simplify \\(a^5a^3\\).","answer":"\\(a^8\\)","solution":"Equal bases under multiplication give \\(a^{5+3}=a^8\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(a^8\\)","\\(a^{15}\\)","\\(2a^8\\)","\\(a^2\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F02","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Simplify \\(\\dfrac{x^9}{x^4}\\), for \\(x\\ne0\\).","answer":"\\(x^5\\)","solution":"Subtract exponents: \\(9-4=5\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(x^5\\)","\\(x^{13}\\)","\\(x^{-5}\\)","\\(5x\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F03","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Simplify \\((m^3)^4\\).","answer":"\\(m^{12}\\)","solution":"A power of a power multiplies exponents: \\(3\\cdot4=12\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(m^{12}\\)","\\(m^7\\)","\\(4m^3\\)","\\(m^{81}\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F04","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Simplify \\((2a^2b)^3\\).","answer":"\\(8a^6b^3\\)","solution":"Cube every factor: \\(2^3(a^2)^3b^3=8a^6b^3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(8a^6b^3\\)","\\(6a^5b^3\\)","\\(8a^5b\\)","\\(2a^6b^3\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F05","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(7^0\\).","answer":"\\(1\\)","solution":"For a non-zero base, \\(a^0=1\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F06","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(5^{-2}\\).","answer":"\\(\\frac1{25}\\)","solution":"A negative exponent gives the reciprocal: \\(5^{-2}=1/25=0.04\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.04,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F07","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\left(\\dfrac34\\right)^{-2}\\).","answer":"\\(\\frac{16}{9}\\)","solution":"Reciprocate and square: \\((4/3)^2=16/9\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.7777777777777777,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F08","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(27^{2/3}\\).","answer":"\\(9\\)","solution":"The cube root of \\(27\\) is \\(3\\), then \\(3^2=9\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":9.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F09","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(16^{-3/4}\\).","answer":"\\(\\frac18\\)","solution":"The fourth root of \\(16\\) is \\(2\\); \\(2^{-3}=1/8\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.125,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F10","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(81^{1/2}\\).","answer":"\\(9\\)","solution":"A power of \\(1/2\\) is a square root: \\(\\sqrt{81}=9\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":9.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F11","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Rewrite \\(2^5=32\\) in logarithmic form.","answer":"\\(\\log_2 32=5\\)","solution":"The base remains \\(2\\); the exponent \\(5\\) becomes the logarithm value.","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(\\log_2 32=5\\)","\\(\\log_5 32=2\\)","\\(\\log_{32}2=5\\)","\\(\\log_2 5=32\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F12","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Rewrite \\(\\log_3 81=4\\) in exponential form.","answer":"\\(3^4=81\\)","solution":"Logarithmic form \\(\\log_bx=y\\) means \\(b^y=x\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(3^4=81\\)","\\(4^3=81\\)","\\(81^3=4\\)","\\(3^{81}=4\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F13","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_{10}(0.001)\\).","answer":"\\(-3\\)","solution":"Since \\(10^{-3}=0.001\\), the logarithm equals \\(-3\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-3.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F14","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_2(1/8)\\).","answer":"\\(-3\\)","solution":"Since \\(2^{-3}=1/8\\), the value is \\(-3\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-3.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F15","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\ln(e^4)\\).","answer":"\\(4\\)","solution":"Natural logarithm and the exponential base \\(e\\) are inverse operations.","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":4.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F16","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_5(1)\\).","answer":"\\(0\\)","solution":"Any valid base raised to \\(0\\) is \\(1\\), so \\(\\log_5(1)=0\\).","marks":1,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F17","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Which logarithm is defined in the real numbers?","answer":"\\(\\log_{1/2}(8)\\)","solution":"A real logarithm requires a positive base not equal to \\(1\\) and a positive argument.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(\\log_{-2}(8)\\)","\\(\\log_1(8)\\)","\\(\\log_2(-8)\\)","\\(\\log_{1/2}(8)\\)"],"correct":3},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F18","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Find the boundary value for the domain of \\(\\log(2x-10)\\).","answer":"\\(5\\)","solution":"Require \\(2x-10\\gt 0\\), so \\(x\\gt 5\\); the boundary is \\(5\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":5.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F19","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_7(100)\\) to three decimal places.","answer":"\\(2.367\\)","solution":"Use change of base: \\(\\log_7(100)=\\log(100)/\\log(7)\\approx2.367\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.366589324909877,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F20","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Which expression equals \\(\\log_7(20)\\)?","answer":"\\(\\dfrac{\\log20}{\\log7}\\)","solution":"Change of base gives \\(\\log_bx=\\log x/\\log b\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(\\dfrac{\\log20}{\\log7}\\)","\\(\\dfrac{\\log7}{\\log20}\\)","\\(\\log(20/7)\\)","\\(20\\log7\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F21","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Solve \\(4^x=64\\).","answer":"\\(3\\)","solution":"Write \\(64=4^3\\), so equal bases give \\(x=3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":3.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F22","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Solve \\(9^{x-1}=3^{x+3}\\).","answer":"\\(5\\)","solution":"Rewrite \\(9=3^2\\): \\(3^{2x-2}=3^{x+3}\\), so \\(x=5\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":5.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F23","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Solve \\(\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{2x}=8\\).","answer":"\\(-\\frac32\\)","solution":"Write \\(2^{-2x}=2^3\\), so \\(-2x=3\\) and \\(x=-3/2\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-1.5,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-F24","level":"Foundation","prompt":"Which statement is false in general?","answer":"\\((a+b)^n=a^n+b^n\\)","solution":"Exponent laws distribute across products, not across sums.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\((ab)^n=a^nb^n\\)","\\(\\dfrac{a^m}{a^n}=a^{m-n}\\)","\\((a+b)^n=a^n+b^n\\)","\\((a^m)^n=a^{mn}\\)"],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A01","level":"Application","prompt":"Simplify \\(\\dfrac{12x^7y^{-2}}{3x^{-1}y^4}\\), for \\(x,y\\ne0\\).","answer":"\\(\\dfrac{4x^8}{y^6}\\)","solution":"Divide coefficients and subtract exponents: \\(x^{7-(-1)}=x^8\\), \\(y^{-2-4}=y^{-6}\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(\\dfrac{4x^8}{y^6}\\)","\\(4x^6y^2\\)","\\(\\dfrac{4x^6}{y^2}\\)","\\(9x^8y^2\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A02","level":"Application","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\left(\\dfrac{64}{125}\\right)^{2/3}\\) exactly.","answer":"\\(\\frac{16}{25}\\)","solution":"Take cube roots first: \\((4/5)^2=16/25\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.64,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A03","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(4^{3x-1}=8^{x+2}\\).","answer":"\\(\\frac83\\)","solution":"Write both sides in base \\(2\\): \\(2^{6x-2}=2^{3x+6}\\), so \\(3x=8\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.6666666666666665,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A04","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(25^{x+1}=5^{3x-2}\\).","answer":"\\(4\\)","solution":"Since \\(25=5^2\\), \\(2x+2=3x-2\\), giving \\(x=4\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":4.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A05","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(2^x+2^{x+1}=96\\).","answer":"\\(5\\)","solution":"Factor \\(2^x(1+2)=96\\), so \\(2^x=32=2^5\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":5.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A06","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(3^{2x-1}=17\\), giving \\(x\\) to three decimal places.","answer":"\\(1.789\\)","solution":"Take logs: \\((2x-1)\\ln3=\\ln17\\), so \\(x=(1+\\ln17/\\ln3)/2\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.7894509615812828,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A07","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(5(2^x)=70\\), giving \\(x\\) to three decimal places.","answer":"\\(3.807\\)","solution":"Isolate \\(2^x=14\\), then \\(x=\\ln14/\\ln2\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":3.8073549220576037,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A08","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(120(1.12)^t=300\\), giving \\(t\\) to two decimal places.","answer":"\\(8.09\\)","solution":"Divide by \\(120\\) and use \\(t=\\ln(2.5)/\\ln(1.12)\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":8.0852498146604,"tolerance":0.005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A09","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(800(0.84)^t=250\\), giving \\(t\\) to two decimal places.","answer":"\\(6.67\\)","solution":"Isolate \\(0.84^t=250/800\\), then take logarithms.","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":6.671225772286463,"tolerance":0.005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A10","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(e^{0.035t}=2\\), giving \\(t\\) to two decimal places.","answer":"\\(19.80\\)","solution":"Take natural logs: \\(0.035t=\\ln2\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":19.804205158855577,"tolerance":0.005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A11","level":"Application","prompt":"Solve \\(5e^{0.4x}-2=18\\), giving \\(x\\) to two decimal places.","answer":"\\(3.47\\)","solution":"Isolate \\(e^{0.4x}=4\\), so \\(x=\\ln4/0.4\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":3.465735902799726,"tolerance":0.005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A12","level":"Application","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_3(20)\\) to three decimal places.","answer":"\\(2.727\\)","solution":"Use change of base: \\(\\log_3(20)=\\ln20/\\ln3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.7268330278608417,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A13","level":"Application","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_{1/2}(8)\\).","answer":"\\(-3\\)","solution":"\\((1/2)^{-3}=2^3=8\\), so the logarithm is \\(-3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-3.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A14","level":"Application","prompt":"Find the boundary value for the domain of \\(\\log(5-2x)\\).","answer":"\\(2.5\\)","solution":"Require \\(5-2x\\gt 0\\), so \\(x\\lt 2.5\\); the boundary is \\(2.5\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.5,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A15","level":"Application","prompt":"Given \\(\\log A=2.3\\) and \\(\\log B=0.8\\), find \\(\\log(AB)\\).","answer":"\\(3.1\\)","solution":"Use the product law: \\(\\log(AB)=\\log A+\\log B=3.1\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":3.1,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A16","level":"Application","prompt":"Given \\(\\log A=2.3\\) and \\(\\log B=0.8\\), find \\(\\log(A/B)\\).","answer":"\\(1.5\\)","solution":"Use the quotient law: \\(2.3-0.8=1.5\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.5,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A17","level":"Application","prompt":"Given \\(\\log A=2.3\\), find \\(\\log(A^3)\\).","answer":"\\(6.9\\)","solution":"Use the power law: \\(\\log(A^3)=3\\log A=6.9\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":6.9,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A18","level":"Application","prompt":"Two sound levels differ by \\(20\\) dB. Find the ratio of their intensities.","answer":"\\(100\\)","solution":"Use \\(I_2/I_1=10^{20/10}=10^2=100\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":100.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A19","level":"Application","prompt":"A solution has pH \\(5.6\\). Find \\([H^+]\\) in mol dm\\(^{-3}\\).","answer":"\\(2.51\\times10^{-6}\\)","solution":"From \\(\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log_{10}[H^+]\\), \\([H^+]=10^{-5.6}\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.5118864315095823e-06,"tolerance":5e-09}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A20","level":"Application","prompt":"Two measurements differ by \\(1.7\\) units on a base-10 logarithmic scale. Find the multiplicative ratio.","answer":"\\(50.119\\)","solution":"A scale difference \\(d\\) corresponds to factor \\(10^d\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":50.11872336272722,"tolerance":0.05}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A21","level":"Application","prompt":"Find the doubling time for an amount growing by \\(4.8\\%\\) per year.","answer":"\\(14.78\\text{ years}\\)","solution":"Solve \\(1.048^t=2\\): \\(t=\\ln2/\\ln1.048\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":14.784431849035263,"tolerance":0.02}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A22","level":"Application","prompt":"A value decreases by \\(12\\%\\) each year. Find its half-life.","answer":"\\(5.42\\text{ years}\\)","solution":"Solve \\(0.88^t=0.5\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":5.422270979580217,"tolerance":0.01}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A23","level":"Application","prompt":"A quantity doubles every \\(7.5\\) years. Find the continuous rate constant \\(k\\) in \\(Q=Q_0e^{kt}\\).","answer":"\\(0.09242\\)","solution":"Set \\(e^{7.5k}=2\\), so \\(k=\\ln2/7.5\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.09241962407465937,"tolerance":5e-05}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-A24","level":"Application","prompt":"For \\(P(t)=12000(0.97)^t\\), find the first whole year for which \\(P\\lt 9000\\).","answer":"\\(10\\)","solution":"The continuous crossing is \\(t\\approx9.45\\). Checking years \\(9\\) and \\(10\\) shows the first whole year below the threshold is \\(10\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":10.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R01","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Solve \\(2^{3x-1}=8^{x+2}\\).","answer":"No solution","solution":"Rewrite \\(8^{x+2}=2^{3x+6}\\). Equal bases would require \\(3x-1=3x+6\\), an impossibility.","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(x=-\\frac73\\)","\\(x=0\\)","No solution","All real \\(x\\)"],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R02","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Solve \\(4^{x+1}=2^{2x+2}\\).","answer":"All real \\(x\\)","solution":"Since \\(4^{x+1}=2^{2x+2}\\), the two sides are identical for every real \\(x\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(x=0\\)","\\(x=1\\)","No solution","All real \\(x\\)"],"correct":3},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R03","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"A student solves \\(3^x=5^{x-1}\\) by writing \\(x=x-1\\). What is the error?","answer":"The bases are not equal, so the exponents cannot be equated directly.","solution":"Equal exponents may be inferred only after both sides are expressed using the same valid base.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["The bases are not equal, so the exponents cannot be equated directly.","The exponent law requires \\(x\\gt 0\\).","The equation has no logarithms.","The right side should be \\(5x-1\\)."],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R04","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Explain why \\(a^0=1\\) requires \\(a\\ne0\\).","answer":"The proof uses \\(a^m/a^m=1\\), which is division by a non-zero quantity.","solution":"For \\(a\\ne0\\), \\(a^m/a^m=1\\) and the quotient law gives \\(a^{m-m}=a^0\\). When \\(a=0\\), the quotient is undefined.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Explain","hint":"","tags":[]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R05","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Which expression is not real under the usual real-number interpretation?","answer":"\\((-8)^{1/2}\\)","solution":"An even root of a negative real number is not real. Cube-root expressions are real.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\((-8)^{1/3}\\)","\\((-8)^{2/3}\\)","\\((-8)^{1/2}\\)","\\(8^{-2/3}\\)"],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R06","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Show two valid exact methods for evaluating \\(16^{3/4}\\).","answer":"Root first: \\((\\sqrt[4]{16})^3=2^3=8\\). Power first: \\(\\sqrt[4]{16^3}=8\\).","solution":"Both forms follow \\(a^{m/n}=(\\sqrt[n]{a})^m=\\sqrt[n]{a^m}\\); root first is more efficient here.","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Explain","hint":"","tags":[]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R07","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"A student claims \\(\\log(20+30)=\\log20+\\log30\\). Find the numerical difference (right side minus left side).","answer":"\\(1.079\\)","solution":"The sum law does not exist. Direct evaluation gives the non-zero difference.","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.079181246047625,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R08","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Why must the argument of a real logarithm be positive?","answer":"A positive real base raised to a real power is always positive.","solution":"If \\(b\\gt 0\\), then \\(b^y\\gt 0\\) for every real \\(y\\), so no real exponent produces zero or a negative result.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["A positive real base raised to a real power is always positive.","Logarithms may only be used with integers.","A logarithm always has a positive answer.","The base and argument must be equal."],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R09","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"If \\(0\\lt x\\lt 1\\), what can be said about \\(\\log_{10}x\\)?","answer":"It is negative.","solution":"Powers \\(10^y\\) lie between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) when \\(y\\lt 0\\), so the logarithm is negative.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["It is positive.","It is zero.","It is negative.","It is undefined."],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R10","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Which is the most efficient first method for \\(27^{2x-1}=9^{x+3}\\)?","answer":"Rewrite both sides in base \\(3\\).","solution":"Both bases are exact powers of \\(3\\), so a common-base method is exact and shortest.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["Rewrite both sides in base \\(3\\).","Use a decimal graph only.","Expand both powers by repeated multiplication.","Take square roots of both sides."],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R11","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"An increasing model crosses a target at \\(t=8.12\\) months. Observations occur at whole months. Find the first recorded month at or above the target.","answer":"\\(9\\)","solution":"The crossing lies between months \\(8\\) and \\(9\\). The first admissible whole-month observation at or above the target is month \\(9\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":9.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R12","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"State the evidence needed to justify that year \\(10\\) is the first whole year below a decreasing threshold.","answer":"Show the continuous boundary and verify year \\(9\\) is not below while year \\(10\\) is below.","solution":"A complete threshold argument checks the adjacent admissible values, not only a rounded decimal crossing.","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Explain","hint":"","tags":[]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R13","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"On a base-10 logarithmic axis, the points \\(10\\), \\(100\\) and \\(1000\\) are equally spaced. Why?","answer":"Their ratios are equal.","solution":"Each successive value is multiplied by \\(10\\); equal log-axis steps represent equal ratios.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["Their ratios are equal.","Their differences are equal.","Their square roots are equal.","Their logarithms are all zero."],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R14","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Two sound levels differ by \\(30\\) dB. Find their intensity ratio.","answer":"\\(1000\\)","solution":"Use \\(10^{30/10}=10^3=1000\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1000.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R15","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Solution A has pH \\(6\\) and solution B has pH \\(4\\). How many times greater is \\([H^+]\\) in B than in A?","answer":"\\(100\\)","solution":"A decrease of \\(2\\) pH units multiplies hydrogen-ion concentration by \\(10^2=100\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":100.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R16","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"For \\(y=Ab^t\\), which graph should be linear if the model is exact?","answer":"\\(\\log y\\) against \\(t\\)","solution":"Taking logs gives \\(\\log y=\\log A+t\\log b\\), a linear relation in \\(t\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(y\\) against \\(t\\)","\\(\\log y\\) against \\(t\\)","\\(1/y\\) against \\(t^2\\)","\\(y^2\\) against \\(\\log t\\)"],"correct":1},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R17","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"A base-10 scale value increases by \\(1\\). What happens to the underlying positive ratio?","answer":"It is multiplied by \\(10\\).","solution":"An increase of \\(1\\) gives factor \\(10^1=10\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["It increases by \\(1\\).","It doubles.","It is multiplied by \\(10\\).","It is squared."],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R18","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"In \\(N(t)=650(1.14)^t\\), what does \\(1.14\\) mean?","answer":"An increase of \\(14\\%\\) of the current amount each hour.","solution":"A discrete factor \\(1+r=1.14\\) means \\(r=0.14\\), or \\(14\\%\\) growth per hour.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["An increase of \\(1.14\\) cells each hour.","An increase of \\(14\\%\\) of the current amount each hour.","A total increase of \\(14\\%\\).","A continuous rate constant of \\(1.14\\)."],"correct":1},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R19","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"For \\(0\\lt b\\lt 1\\), how does \\(y=\\log_bx\\) change as \\(x\\) increases?","answer":"It decreases.","solution":"The inverse of a decreasing exponential \\(b^y\\) with \\(0\\lt b\\lt 1\\) is also decreasing.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["It increases.","It decreases.","It remains constant.","It is never real."],"correct":1},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R20","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Which set is the domain of \\(\\log\\!\\left(\\dfrac{x-1}{x+2}\\right)\\)?","answer":"\\((-\\infty,-2)\\cup(1,\\infty)\\)","solution":"Require \\((x-1)/(x+2)\\gt 0\\). The quotient is positive when numerator and denominator have the same sign: \\(x\\lt -2\\) or \\(x\\gt 1\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\((-2,1)\\)","\\((-\\infty,-2)\\cup(1,\\infty)\\)","\\((-2,\\infty)\\)","\\((1,\\infty)\\)"],"correct":1},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R21","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"If \\(\\log_2x=5\\), find \\(\\log_2(x/4)\\).","answer":"\\(3\\)","solution":"\\(\\log_2(x/4)=\\log_2x-\\log_24=5-2=3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":3.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R22","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Before calculating, between which consecutive integers must the solution of \\(2^x=30\\) lie?","answer":"\\(4\\) and \\(5\\)","solution":"\\(2^4=16\\lt 30\\lt 32=2^5\\), so \\(4\\lt x\\lt 5\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(2\\) and \\(3\\)","\\(3\\) and \\(4\\)","\\(4\\) and \\(5\\)","\\(5\\) and \\(6\\)"],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R23","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"Explain why the doubling time decreases when a positive percentage growth rate increases.","answer":"A larger growth factor reaches the same factor \\(2\\) in fewer periods.","solution":"Since \\(t=\\ln2/\\ln(1+r)\\), increasing \\(r\\) increases the positive denominator, so the quotient decreases.","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Explain","hint":"","tags":[]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-R24","level":"Reasoning","prompt":"State one mathematical limitation of extending \\(N(t)=650(1.14)^t\\) indefinitely.","answer":"The constant \\(14\\%\\) growth factor is unlikely to persist because resources or capacity are finite.","solution":"Exponential models assume a constant proportional rate. In real systems, saturation, competition or changing conditions eventually alter that rate.","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Explain","hint":"","tags":[]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C01","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(2^x+2^{1-x}=5\\).","answer":"\\(x\\approx2.190\\text{ or }x\\approx-1.190\\)","solution":"Let \\(u=2^x\\gt 0\\). Then \\(u+2/u=5\\), so \\(u^2-5u+2=0\\). Both positive roots \\(u=(5\\pm\\sqrt{17})/2\\) produce real logarithmic values of \\(x\\).","marks":5,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(x\\approx2.190\\) only","\\(x\\approx-1.190\\) only","\\(x\\approx2.190\\text{ or }x\\approx-1.190\\)","No real solution"],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C02","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(9^x-10\\cdot3^x+9=0\\).","answer":"\\(x=0\\text{ or }x=2\\)","solution":"Let \\(u=3^x\\gt 0\\). Then \\(u^2-10u+9=0\\), so \\(u=1\\) or \\(u=9\\), giving \\(x=0\\) or \\(x=2\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\(x=0\\)","\\(x=2\\)","\\(x=0\\text{ or }x=2\\)","No real solution"],"correct":2},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C03","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(4^x+2^{x+1}-8=0\\).","answer":"\\(1\\)","solution":"Let \\(u=2^x\\gt 0\\). Then \\(u^2+2u-8=0\\), so \\(u=2\\) (reject \\(u=-4\\)); hence \\(x=1\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C04","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A model \\(Q=Ab^t\\) satisfies \\(Q(2)=400\\) and \\(Q(7)=900\\). Find \\(b\\).","answer":"\\(1.1761\\)","solution":"Divide the equations: \\(b^5=900/400=2.25\\), so \\(b=2.25^{1/5}\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.1760790225246736,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C05","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A quantity grows by a factor of \\(6\\) in \\(15\\) time units at a constant exponential rate. Find the time required to grow by a factor of \\(10\\).","answer":"\\(19.28\\)","solution":"If the growth factor is \\(e^{kt}\\), then \\(15k=\\ln6\\). The required time is \\(\\ln10/k=15\\ln10/\\ln6\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":19.276458134077036,"tolerance":0.02}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C06","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Find the percentage increase in the underlying quantity when a base-10 log-scale value rises from \\(3.4\\) to \\(5.1\\).","answer":"\\(4911.9\\%\\)","solution":"The scale difference is \\(1.7\\), so the multiplicative factor is \\(10^{1.7}\\). Percentage increase is \\((10^{1.7}-1)100\\%\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":4911.872336272722,"tolerance":5}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C07","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A model is \\(y=4.2+1.6\\log_{10}x\\). Find \\(x\\) when \\(y=10\\).","answer":"\\(4216.97\\)","solution":"Rearrange to \\(\\log_{10}x=(10-4.2)/1.6=3.625\\), then \\(x=10^{3.625}\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":4216.965034285819,"tolerance":5}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C08","level":"Challenge","prompt":"An exponential model satisfies \\(Q(0)=250\\) and \\(Q(6)=700\\). Find the discrete growth factor \\(b\\) in \\(Q=250b^t\\).","answer":"\\(1.1872\\)","solution":"Use \\(700=250b^6\\), so \\(b=(700/250)^{1/6}\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.1872066991105543,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C09","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A medicine retains \\(40\\%\\) of its amount after \\(6\\) hours. Find the hourly retention factor.","answer":"\\(0.8584\\)","solution":"If the hourly factor is \\(r\\), then \\(r^6=0.40\\), so \\(r=0.40^{1/6}\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.858374218932557,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C10","level":"Challenge","prompt":"If \\(\\log_bx=-2\\), express \\(x\\) as a power of \\(b\\). Enter the exponent of \\(b\\).","answer":"\\(-2\\)","solution":"By definition, \\(\\log_bx=-2\\) means \\(b^{-2}=x\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-2.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C11","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(\\log_2(x-1)=3\\).","answer":"\\(9\\)","solution":"Convert to exponential form: \\(x-1=2^3=8\\), so \\(x=9\\), which satisfies the domain \\(x\\gt 1\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":9.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C12","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(\\log_{10}x+\\log_{10}(x-9)=1\\).","answer":"\\(10\\)","solution":"Domain requires \\(x\\gt 9\\). Combine logs: \\(x(x-9)=10\\), giving \\(x=10\\) or \\(-1\\); reject \\(-1\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":10.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C13","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(2\\ln x=\\ln49\\).","answer":"\\(7\\)","solution":"Use \\(2\\ln x=\\ln(x^2)\\). Then \\(x^2=49\\), but the logarithm domain requires \\(x\\gt 0\\), so \\(x=7\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":7.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C14","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Solve \\(\\log_3x+\\log_3(x-2)=2\\).","answer":"\\(1+\\sqrt{10}\\approx4.162\\)","solution":"Domain requires \\(x\\gt 2\\). Combine logs: \\(x(x-2)=9\\), so \\(x=1\\pm\\sqrt{10}\\); only \\(1+\\sqrt{10}\\) is admissible.","marks":5,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":4.16227766016838,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C15","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Find the domain of \\(\\log((x-1)(x+3))\\).","answer":"\\((-\\infty,-3)\\cup(1,\\infty)\\)","solution":"Require \\((x-1)(x+3)\\gt 0\\). A product of two linear factors is positive outside its roots.","marks":4,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":[],"choices":["\\((-3,1)\\)","\\((-\\infty,-3)\\cup(1,\\infty)\\)","\\((-3,\\infty)\\)","\\((1,\\infty)\\)"],"correct":1},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C16","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Two measurements differ by \\(2.4\\) on a base-10 logarithmic scale. Find their multiplicative ratio.","answer":"\\(251.19\\)","solution":"Use factor \\(10^{2.4}\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":251.18864315095797,"tolerance":0.2}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C17","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Sound intensity is \\(5.0\\times10^6\\) times the reference intensity. Find the sound level \\(L=10\\log_{10}(I/I_0)\\).","answer":"\\(66.99\\text{ dB}\\)","solution":"Substitute the ratio: \\(L=10\\log_{10}(5.0\\times10^6)\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":66.98970004336019,"tolerance":0.02}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C18","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A solution has \\([H^+]=2.5\\times10^{-6}\\) mol dm\\(^{-3}\\). Find its pH.","answer":"\\(5.602\\)","solution":"Use \\(\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log_{10}(2.5\\times10^{-6})\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":5.6020599913279625,"tolerance":0.002}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C19","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A quantity follows \\(Q=Q_0e^{kt}\\) and retains \\(65\\%\\) after \\(8\\) hours. Find \\(k\\).","answer":"\\(-0.05385\\)","solution":"Use \\(0.65=e^{8k}\\), so \\(k=\\ln(0.65)/8\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-0.05384786451155678,"tolerance":5e-05}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C20","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Using the model in the previous question, find the time to reach \\(10\\%\\) of the initial amount.","answer":"\\(42.76\\text{ h}\\)","solution":"Solve \\(0.10=e^{kt}\\), so \\(t=\\ln(0.10)/k\\). Both numerator and \\(k\\) are negative, giving positive time.","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":42.760936090601454,"tolerance":0.05}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C21","level":"Challenge","prompt":"Plan A grows as \\(A=500(1.08)^t\\) and Plan B as \\(B=700(1.04)^t\\). Find when their values are equal.","answer":"\\(8.92\\)","solution":"Set \\(500(1.08)^t=700(1.04)^t\\), so \\((1.08/1.04)^t=1.4\\).","marks":5,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":8.915456081201485,"tolerance":0.02}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C22","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A culture follows \\(N=300(1.11)^t\\). Find the first whole hour at which \\(N\\gt 1200\\).","answer":"\\(14\\)","solution":"The continuous crossing is \\(t=\\ln4/\\ln1.11\\approx13.28\\). Check hours \\(13\\) and \\(14\\); the first whole hour above the target is \\(14\\).","marks":5,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":14.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C23","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A graph of \\(\\log_{10}y\\) against \\(t\\) has slope \\(0.075\\) for data modelled by \\(y=Ab^t\\). Find \\(b\\).","answer":"\\(1.1885\\)","solution":"Taking logs gives \\(\\log_{10}y=\\log_{10}A+t\\log_{10}b\\), so \\(\\log_{10}b=0.075\\) and \\(b=10^{0.075}\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":[],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.1885022274370185,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-C24","level":"Challenge","prompt":"A straight line appears when \\(\\log_{10}y\\) is plotted against \\(t\\), but the residuals curve systematically. What does this suggest?","answer":"The exponential model captures a broad trend but the growth factor is not constant across the full interval.","solution":"Curved residual structure is non-random evidence of model misspecification. A piecewise model, saturation model or changing rate may be more appropriate.","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Comment","hint":"","tags":[]}];
data.quiz=[{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q01","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Evaluate \\(64^{-2/3}\\).","answer":"\\(\\frac1{16}\\)","solution":"The cube root of \\(64\\) is \\(4\\); \\(4^{-2}=1/16\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":0.0625,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q02","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Simplify \\(\\dfrac{(a^4)^3}{a^5}\\), for \\(a\\ne0\\).","answer":"\\(a^7\\)","solution":"\\((a^4)^3=a^{12}\\), then \\(a^{12}/a^5=a^7\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"choices":["\\(a^7\\)","\\(a^{17}\\)","\\(a^{-7}\\)","\\(3a^{-1}\\)"],"correct":0},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q03","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Solve \\(8^{x-1}=4^{x+2}\\).","answer":"\\(7\\)","solution":"Write \\(2^{3x-3}=2^{2x+4}\\), so \\(x=7\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":7.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q04","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Solve \\(4^{2x+1}=8^{x+2}\\).","answer":"\\(x=4\\)","solution":"Write \\(2^{4x+2}=2^{3x+6}\\), so \\(x=4\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Select","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"choices":["\\(x=1\\)","\\(x=4\\)","No solution","All real \\(x\\)"],"correct":1},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q05","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_4(1/64)\\).","answer":"\\(-3\\)","solution":"Since \\(4^{-3}=1/64\\), the logarithm is \\(-3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":-3.0,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q06","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Evaluate \\(\\log_6(45)\\) to three decimal places.","answer":"\\(2.125\\)","solution":"Use change of base: \\(\\ln45/\\ln6\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.124538787234844,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q07","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Solve \\(240(1.09)^t=900\\), giving \\(t\\) to two decimal places.","answer":"\\(15.34\\)","solution":"Isolate the exponential factor and take logarithms.","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":15.337562938386784,"tolerance":0.005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q08","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Solve \\(500(0.82)^t=120\\), giving \\(t\\) to two decimal places.","answer":"\\(7.19\\)","solution":"Use \\(t=\\ln(120/500)/\\ln0.82\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":7.191280448545028,"tolerance":0.005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q09","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Find the boundary value for the domain of \\(\\ln(7-3x)\\).","answer":"\\(\\frac73\\)","solution":"Require \\(7-3x\\gt 0\\), so \\(x\\lt 7/3\\); the boundary is \\(7/3\\).","marks":2,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.3333333333333335,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q10","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Two sound levels differ by \\(15\\) dB. Find the intensity ratio.","answer":"\\(31.62\\)","solution":"Use \\(10^{15/10}=10^{1.5}\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":31.622776601683793,"tolerance":0.02}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q11","level":"Quiz","prompt":"A solution has \\([H^+]=4.0\\times10^{-5}\\) mol dm\\(^{-3}\\). Find its pH.","answer":"\\(4.398\\)","solution":"Use \\(\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log_{10}(4.0\\times10^{-5})\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":4.3979400086720375,"tolerance":0.002}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q12","level":"Quiz","prompt":"Given \\(\\ln A=1.6\\) and \\(\\ln B=0.3\\), find \\(\\ln(A^2/B)\\).","answer":"\\(2.9\\)","solution":"Use the power and quotient laws: \\(2\\ln A-\\ln B=2.9\\).","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":2.9000000000000004,"tolerance":1e-06}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q13","level":"Quiz","prompt":"A model \\(Q=Ab^t\\) satisfies \\(Q(1)=180\\) and \\(Q(5)=420\\). Find \\(b\\).","answer":"\\(1.2359\\)","solution":"Divide the equations to obtain \\(b^4=420/180\\).","marks":4,"calculator":"GDC expected","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":1.235930917022447,"tolerance":0.0005}},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-Q14","level":"Quiz","prompt":"A decreasing model crosses a threshold at \\(t=12.23\\) days. Measurements are recorded at whole days. Find the first recorded day below the threshold.","answer":"\\(13\\)","solution":"The crossing occurs between days \\(12\\) and \\(13\\); the first whole-day observation below is day \\(13\\), subject to adjacent-value verification.","marks":3,"calculator":"No calculator","command":"Calculate","hint":"","tags":["quiz"],"check":{"mode":"number","value":13.0,"tolerance":1e-06}}];
data.exam=[{"id":"ELV6-1.5-E01","style":"Paper 1-style · no calculator","title":"Exponent structure and exact reasoning","calculator":"No calculator","total_marks":10,"context":"Let \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) be non-zero real numbers where every stated rational power is real.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Simplify \\(\\dfrac{(a^3b^{-2})^2a^{-1}}{a^2b^{-5}}\\), writing the answer with positive exponents.","marks":3,"answer":"\\(a^3b\\)","markscheme":"M1 expands the outer power; M1 combines the exponents correctly; A1 obtains \\(a^3b\\)."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Evaluate \\(32^{-3/5}\\) exactly.","marks":2,"answer":"\\(\\frac18\\)","markscheme":"M1 uses the fifth root \\(32^{1/5}=2\\); A1 gives \\(2^{-3}=1/8\\)."},{"label":"c","prompt":"Solve \\(9^{x-1}=27^{2-x}\\).","marks":3,"answer":"\\(x=\\frac85\\)","markscheme":"M1 rewrites both sides in base \\(3\\); M1 solves \\(2x-2=6-3x\\); A1 gives \\(x=8/5\\)."},{"label":"d","prompt":"A student states that \\((p+q)^3=p^3+q^3\\). Disprove the statement with a counterexample.","marks":2,"answer":"For \\(p=q=1\\), the two sides are \\(8\\) and \\(2\\), so the identity is false.","markscheme":"M1 chooses a valid counterexample; A1 evaluates both sides and concludes the claim is false."}]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-E02","style":"Paper 2-style · modelling","title":"Culture threshold","calculator":"GDC expected","total_marks":11,"context":"A culture is modelled by \\(N(t)=650(1.14)^t\\), where \\(t\\) is measured in hours.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Calculate \\(N(6)\\), giving the answer to the nearest whole cell.","marks":2,"answer":"1427 cells","markscheme":"M1 substitutes \\(t=6\\); A1 gives \\(1427\\) cells."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Determine the time when the model reaches \\(3000\\) cells.","marks":3,"answer":"\\(t\\approx11.672\\) hours","markscheme":"M1 isolates \\(1.14^t=3000/650\\); M1 uses logarithms; A1 gives \\(t\u0007pprox11.672\\)."},{"label":"c","prompt":"Observations are recorded at the end of each whole hour. Find the first observation time at which the model exceeds \\(3000\\) cells.","marks":2,"answer":"12 hours","markscheme":"M1 interprets the continuous crossing and/or checks adjacent hours; A1 states \\(12\\) hours."},{"label":"d","prompt":"Interpret the factor \\(1.14\\) in context.","marks":2,"answer":"The model increases by \\(14\\%\\) of the current population each hour.","markscheme":"A1 states \\(14\\%\\); A1 links the percentage to each hour and the current amount."},{"label":"e","prompt":"State one reason why this model should not be extended indefinitely.","marks":2,"answer":"Resources or capacity eventually limit the growth, so a constant \\(14\\%\\) rate is unlikely to persist.","markscheme":"A1 gives a valid limitation; A1 links it to failure of the constant proportional-growth assumption."}]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-E03","style":"Paper 2-style · decay and threshold","title":"Medicine concentration","calculator":"GDC expected","total_marks":11,"context":"The amount of a medicine is modelled by \\(M(t)=1200(0.86)^t\\) milligrams, where \\(t\\) is measured in hours.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Calculate \\(M(8)\\), giving the answer to the nearest milligram.","marks":2,"answer":"359 mg","markscheme":"M1 substitutes \\(t=8\\); A1 gives \\(359\\) mg."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Determine when the amount reaches \\(300\\) mg.","marks":3,"answer":"\\(t\\approx9.192\\) hours","markscheme":"M1 isolates \\(0.86^t=0.25\\); M1 applies logarithms; A1 gives \\(t\u0007pprox9.192\\)."},{"label":"c","prompt":"The medicine is measured at whole hours. Find the first whole hour when the amount is below \\(300\\) mg.","marks":2,"answer":"10 hours","markscheme":"M1 checks the continuous boundary or adjacent integer values; A1 states \\(10\\) hours."},{"label":"d","prompt":"Find the percentage decrease per hour represented by the model.","marks":2,"answer":"\\(14\\%\\) decrease per hour","markscheme":"M1 identifies \\(1-0.86=0.14\\); A1 gives \\(14\\%\\) per hour."},{"label":"e","prompt":"Explain why the logarithmic answer in part (b) is positive even though \\(\\ln(0.86)\\) is negative.","marks":2,"answer":"Both \\(\\ln(0.25)\\) and \\(\\ln(0.86)\\) are negative, so their quotient is positive.","markscheme":"A1 identifies both logarithms as negative; A1 explains that the quotient is positive."}]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-E04","style":"Paper 2-style · logarithmic scale","title":"Sound intensity","calculator":"GDC expected","total_marks":12,"context":"Sound level is modelled by \\(L=10\\log_{10}(I/I_0)\\) decibels, where \\(I_0\\gt 0\\) is a reference intensity.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Find \\(L\\) when \\(I=100I_0\\).","marks":2,"answer":"\\(20\\) dB","markscheme":"M1 substitutes \\(I/I_0=100\\); A1 gives \\(10\\log_{10}(100)=20\\)."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Find \\(I/I_0\\) when \\(L=35\\) dB.","marks":3,"answer":"\\(\\dfrac I{I_0}\\approx3162.278\\)","markscheme":"M1 divides by \\(10\\); M1 exponentiates base \\(10\\); A1 gives approximately \\(3162.278\\)."},{"label":"c","prompt":"Two sounds differ by \\(18\\) dB. Find the ratio of their intensities.","marks":2,"answer":"\\(63.096\\)","markscheme":"M1 uses \\(10^{18/10}\\); A1 gives approximately \\(63.096\\)."},{"label":"d","prompt":"Explain why equal additions to \\(L\\) represent multiplicative, not additive, changes in intensity.","marks":3,"answer":"The logarithm converts intensity ratios into differences in level; increasing \\(L\\) by a fixed amount multiplies \\(I/I_0\\) by a fixed power of \\(10\\).","markscheme":"A1 identifies the logarithmic relationship; A1 refers to ratios or multiplication; A1 links a fixed level change to a fixed factor."},{"label":"e","prompt":"State the domain restrictions on \\(I\\) and \\(I_0\\).","marks":2,"answer":"\\(I\\gt 0\\) and \\(I_0\\gt 0\\)","markscheme":"A1 requires \\(I_0\\gt 0\\); A1 requires the logarithm argument \\(I/I_0\\gt 0\\), hence \\(I\\gt 0\\)."}]},{"id":"ELV6-1.5-E05","style":"Paper 2-style · parameter recovery","title":"Reconstructing an exponential model","calculator":"GDC expected","total_marks":12,"context":"A quantity is modelled by \\(Q(t)=Ab^t\\). Measurements give \\(Q(2)=400\\) and \\(Q(7)=900\\).","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Show that \\(b^5=2.25\\).","marks":2,"answer":"\\(\\dfrac{Q(7)}{Q(2)}=b^5=\\dfrac{900}{400}=2.25\\)","markscheme":"M1 divides the two model equations; A1 obtains \\(b^5=2.25\\)."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Find \\(b\\), giving the answer to four decimal places.","marks":2,"answer":"\\(b\\approx1.1761\\)","markscheme":"M1 calculates \\(2.25^{1/5}\\); A1 gives \\(1.1761\\)."},{"label":"c","prompt":"Find \\(A\\), giving the answer to three significant figures.","marks":2,"answer":"\\(A\\approx289\\)","markscheme":"M1 substitutes a known point into \\(Q=Ab^t\\); A1 gives \\(A\u0007pprox289\\)."},{"label":"d","prompt":"Determine when the model first reaches \\(1500\\).","marks":3,"answer":"\\(t\\approx10.150\\)","markscheme":"M1 forms \\(Ab^t=1500\\); M1 applies logarithms; A1 gives \\(t\u0007pprox10.150\\)."},{"label":"e","prompt":"Interpret the value of \\(b\\) in context.","marks":1,"answer":"The quantity is multiplied by about \\(1.1761\\), an increase of about \\(17.61\\%\\), per time unit.","markscheme":"A1 gives a correct multiplicative or percentage interpretation per time unit."},{"label":"f","prompt":"State one check that would help assess whether this model is reliable beyond the two measured points.","marks":2,"answer":"Compare additional observed values with model predictions and inspect residuals for systematic structure.","markscheme":"A1 proposes additional evidence or residual checking; A1 links the check to model reliability."}]}];
